mazu</strong>ã�¯ã€�æ—¥æœ¬èª�ã�«ç‰¹åŒ–ã�—ã�ŸLLMã�§ã�™ã€‚æ—¥æœ¬ã�®æ–‡è„ˆã�«é¦´æŸ“ã‚“ã� çŸ¥èƒ½ã‚’ã€�å°�å…¥ã�—ã‚„ã�™ã�„ä¾¡æ ¼ã�§æ��ä¾›ã�—ã�¾ã�™ã€‚ãƒ¢ãƒ‡ãƒ«ã�®è©³ç´°ã�¯<a href=\"/namazu/\">ã�“ã�¡ã‚‰</a>ã‚’ã�”è¦§ã��ã� ã�•ã�„ã€‚</p>\n\n<p>ç®¡ç�†æ©Ÿèƒ½ã�¨ã�—ã�¦ã�¯ã€�çµ„ç¹”ã�®ä¸‹ã�«ãƒ¯ãƒ¼ã‚¯ã‚¹ãƒšãƒ¼ã‚¹ã‚’ä½œã‚Šã€�ãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã‚„ç’°å¢ƒã�”ã�¨ã�®è¨­å®šã‚’ã�—ã�Ÿã‚Šã€�ãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã�®åˆ©ç”¨çŠ¶æ³�ã‚’æŠŠæ�¡ã�§ã��ã�¾ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><strong>ä¸»ã�ªæ©Ÿèƒ½ä¸€è¦§</strong></p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>çµ„ç¹”ãƒ»ãƒ¯ãƒ¼ã‚¯ã‚¹ãƒšãƒ¼ã‚¹ç®¡ç�†ã�¨ç®¡ç�†ã‚³ãƒ³ã‚½ãƒ¼ãƒ«ï¼ˆãƒ¡ãƒ³ãƒ�ãƒ¼ã€�APIã‚­ãƒ¼ã€�åˆ©ç”¨é‡�ã‚’ä¸€å…ƒç®¡ç�†ï¼‰</li>\n  <li>ãƒ­ãƒ¼ãƒ«ãƒ™ãƒ¼ã‚¹ã�®ã‚¢ã‚¯ã‚»ã‚¹åˆ¶å¾¡</li>\n  <li>ç›£æŸ»ãƒ­ã‚°ï¼ˆCSVãƒ€ã‚¦ãƒ³ãƒ­ãƒ¼ãƒ‰ï¼‰ã€�å…¥åŠ›ãƒ‡ãƒ¼ã‚¿ã‚’å­¦ç¿’ã�«ä½¿ç”¨ã�—ã�ªã�„ã�“ã�¨ã‚’ãƒ‡ãƒ¼ã‚¿å�–æ‰±æ�¡ä»¶ã�¨ã�—ã�¦æ˜�ç¤º</li>\n  <li>äºˆç®—ä¸Šé™�è¨­å®šã€�è«‹æ±‚æ›¸æ‰•ã�„å¯¾å¿œã€�MSAã�Šã‚ˆã�³DPAã€�ã�ªã�©</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>ä»Šå¾Œã‚‚ã€�çµ„ç¹”å�˜ä½�ã�§ã�®è©³ç´°ã�ªæ¨©é™�åˆ¶å¾¡ã�ªã�©ã€�æ©Ÿèƒ½ã‚’æ‹¡å……ã�™ã‚‹äºˆå®šã�§ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"ã‚¢ãƒ¼ãƒªãƒ¼ã‚¢ã‚¯ã‚»ã‚¹ãƒ—ãƒ­ã‚°ãƒ©ãƒ ã�«ã�¤ã�„ã�¦\"><strong>ã‚¢ãƒ¼ãƒªãƒ¼ã‚¢ã‚¯ã‚»ã‚¹ãƒ—ãƒ­ã‚°ãƒ©ãƒ ã�«ã�¤ã�„ã�¦</strong></h2>\n\n<p>ã�Šç”³ã�—è¾¼ã�¿ã�„ã�Ÿã� ã�„ã�Ÿã�Šå®¢æ§˜ã�®ã�†ã�¡ã€�å½“ç¤¾å�´ã�®æº–å‚™ã�Œæ•´ã�£ã�Ÿã�Šå®¢æ§˜ã�‹ã‚‰é †ã�«ã€�ä¸€èˆ¬æ��ä¾›ã�«å…ˆç«‹ã�£ã�¦ã�”åˆ©ç”¨ã�„ã�Ÿã� ã�‘ã�¾ã�™ã€‚ã�Šç”³ã�—è¾¼ã�¿å†…å®¹ã‚’ç¢ºèª�ã�®ã�†ã�ˆã€�å€‹åˆ¥ã�«ã�”é€£çµ¡ã�„ã�Ÿã�—ã�¾ã�™ã€‚æ–™é‡‘ã�Šã‚ˆã�³ã�”åˆ©ç”¨æ�¡ä»¶ã�¯ã€�ã�”æ¡ˆå†…ã�®éš›ã�«ã�Šä¼�ã�ˆã�—ã�¾ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><strong>ğŸ‘‰<a href=\"https://forms.gle/ZaxjS98B3zc84fA69\">ã‚¢ãƒ¼ãƒªãƒ¼ã‚¢ã‚¯ã‚»ã‚¹ãƒ—ãƒ­ã‚°ãƒ©ãƒ ã�«ç”³è¾¼ã‚€</a></strong></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sakana-ai\"><strong>Sakana AI</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Sakana AIã�§ã�¯ã€�Sakana API Platformã�®ã‚ˆã�†ã�ªãƒ—ãƒ­ãƒ€ã‚¯ãƒˆã‚’ã€�ã�Šå®¢ã�•ã�¾ã�®ç�¾å ´ã�«å±Šã�‘ã�¦ã�„ã��ä»²é–“ã‚’å‹Ÿé›†ã�—ã�¦ã�„ã�¾ã�™ã€‚å½“ç¤¾ã�®<a href=\"/careers/\">å‹Ÿé›†è¦�é …</a>ã‚’ã�”è¦§ã��ã� ã�•ã�„ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<a href=\"/careers/\"><img src=\"/assets/azabudai-hills/fish-outside-hills.jpeg\" style=\"width: 80%;\" /></a><br />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<p><h id=\"English\" style=\"color:#FFF;\">English Announcement</h></p>\n\n<h1 id=\"sakana-api-platform-early-access-program-now-open-for-enterprises\">Sakana API Platform: Early Access Program Now Open for Enterprises</h1>\n\n<center>\n<img class=\"b-lazy\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAAAAACH5BAEKAAEALAAAAAABAAEAAAICTAEAOw==\" data-src=\"/assets/api-platform-earlyaccess/console-en.png\" style=\"width: 100%;\" />\n</center>\n\n<p><small>An illustration of the admin console. This image uses demo data and may change without notice.</small></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p>Today, Sakana AI announced the <strong>Sakana API Platform</strong> for enterprise customers and opened its Early Access program. With Sakana API Platform, you can use LLMs from Sakana AI through an API, together with management features. Organizations can manage usage costs and members in one place, by organization and workspace, so enterprises can adopt it with confidence. The API is OpenAI-compatible: if your applications already call an LLM API, you only need to change the endpoint. To apply, please use the link below.</p>\n\n<p><strong>ğŸ‘‰<a href=\"https://forms.gle/ZaxjS98B3zc84fA69\">Apply for Early Access Program</a></strong></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"what-is-the-sakana-api-platform\"><strong>What is the Sakana API Platform?</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Sakana API Platform is the entry point for enterprise customers to use Sakana AIâ€™s models and products in their own operations, calling them from applications and systems. It also provides the management features that enterprise operations require.</p>\n\n<p>Until now, the Fugu and Namazu models have been offered as standalone APIs. Sakana API Platform lets organizations of any size use them under more controlled management, with confidence.</p>\n\n<p>Sakana Fugu and Sakana Fugu Ultra are available first. Sakana Namazu and Sakana Fugu Max will follow.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sakana Fugu</strong> is an orchestration foundation model that orchestrates multiple frontier models. It assembles the combination of models best suited to your workload. It builds on research accepted at ICLR 2026 (Trinity and Conductor). The lineup has three models: Fugu (standard), Fugu Ultra, and Fugu Max. For details, see <a href=\"/fugu/\">here</a>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sakana Namazu</strong> is an LLM specialized in Japanese. It offers intelligence attuned to the Japanese context. For details, see <a href=\"/namazu/\">here</a>.</p>\n\n<p>For management, you can create workspaces under your organization, configure settings for each team or environment, and see how each team is using the platform.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Key features</strong></p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Organization and workspace management, with an admin console (members, API keys, and usage in one place)</li>\n  <li>Role-based access control</li>\n  <li>Audit logs (CSV download), and data handling terms that state your inputs are not used for training</li>\n  <li>Budget limits, invoice payment, MSA and DPA, and more</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>We plan to keep expanding these features, including more granular permission controls at the organization level.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"about-early-access-program\"><strong>About Early Access Program</strong></h2>\n\n<p>We will offer access to applicants as they become ready, and contact each applicant individually after reviewing their application. Pricing and terms of use will be shared at that time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>ğŸ‘‰<a href=\"https://forms.gle/ZaxjS98B3zc84fA69\">Apply for Early Access Program</a></strong></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sakana-ai-1\"><strong>Sakana AI</strong></h2>\n\n<p>At Sakana AI, we are looking for teammates who will bring products like Sakana API Platform to our customers where they work. See our <a href=\"/careers/\">open positions</a>.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<a href=\"/careers/\"><img src=\"/assets/azabudai-hills/fish-outside-hills.jpeg\" style=\"width: 80%;\" /></a><br />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>]]></content><author><name>Sakana AI</name></author><category term=\"media\" /><summary type=\"html\"><![CDATA[ç®¡ç�†ç”»é�¢ã�®ã‚¤ãƒ¡ãƒ¼ã‚¸ã€‚*ã�“ã�®ç”»åƒ�ã�¯æ�¶ç©ºã�®ãƒ‡ãƒ¼ã‚¿ã‚’ä½¿ã�£ã�Ÿã‚¤ãƒ¡ãƒ¼ã‚¸ã�§ã�™ã€‚ä»Šå¾Œäºˆå‘Šã�ªã��å¤‰æ›´ã�•ã‚Œã‚‹å�¯èƒ½æ€§ã�Œã�‚ã‚Šã�¾ã�™ã€‚]]></summary></entry><entry xml:lang=\"en\"><title type=\"html\">Taking Japan-Born AI Products to the World: An Interview with Sakana AIâ€™s VP of GTM</title><link href=\"/vp-of-gtm-interview/\" rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" title=\"Taking Japan-Born AI Products to the World: An Interview with Sakana AIâ€™s VP of GTM\" /><published>2026-10-27T00:00:00+09:00</published><updated>2026-10-27T00:00:00+09:00</updated><id>/vp-of-gtm-interview</id><content type=\"html\" xml:base=\"/vp-of-gtm-interview/\"><![CDATA[<p><img src=\"/assets/vp-of-gtm-interview/thumbnail.jpg\" width=\"100%\" /><br />\n<!--more--></p>\n\n<p>Switch to the Japanese section - <a href=\"#Japanese\">æ—¥æœ¬èª�ç‰ˆã�¯ã�“ã�¡ã‚‰</a></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p>In 2026, Sakana AI released a series of products: Sakana Chat, Sakana Marlin, Sakana Fugu, and Sakana Translate. Known until now as an R&amp;D company, Sakana AI has entered the stage of delivering products born from its research to companies and government agencies. The Go-To-Market (GTM) team that sells and scales those products launched in earnest in September 2026.</p>\n\n<p>Leading the team is Hidekazu Kakishima, who has driven the business launches of new technologies like cloud, IoT, and drones right from the dawn of their respective industries. Why Sakana AI, and why now? How do you sell products that come out of research, and what kind of team do you build to grow them? And what makes it interesting to join a team that still has few established systems? We sat down with him to find out.</p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"interviewee\">Interviewee</h2>\n\n<table style=\"background-color: #FFF;border: 1px solid white;border-collapse: collapse;margin:0;padding:0;\">\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"45%\" style=\"background-color: #FFF;border: 1px solid white;border-collapse: collapse;margin:0;padding:0.5em;\">\n<img src=\"/assets/vp-of-gtm-interview/headshot.jpg\" width=\"100%\" /><br />\n</td>\n<td width=\"55%\" style=\"background-color: #FFF;border: 1px solid white;border-collapse: collapse;margin:0;padding:0.5em;\">\n<b>Hidekazu Kakishima</b><br />VP of GTM<br /><br />\nLed the strategic accounts team during the early days of AWS Japan, then joined IoT connectivity startup SORACOM as a founding member, and later drove the real-world adoption of flying robotics at US drone maker Skydio. He now leads market expansion and value delivery for Sakana AIâ€™s product lineup.\n<p><br /></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"the-frontier-of-technology-draws-talented-people-who-love-whats-new\"><strong>The Frontier of Technology Draws Talented People Who Love Whatâ€™s New</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â€” From AWS to SORACOM, Skydio, and now Sakana AI, you seem to have chosen the early days of a new technology every time. Tell us about your career.</strong></p>\n\n<p>At the root is a belief that technology makes the world better. That is why I have always wanted to work at the leading edge of fields where new technology is emerging. I was not that conscious of it in my twenties, but once I encountered cloud computing at AWS, I switched firmly into that mode.</p>\n\n<p>After that came IoT at SORACOM, drones at Skydio, and now AI. Each is a field where a new industry is being born, and my job is to take the technology at the frontier and put it to work in the market from the inside. That is what I want to do, and I have chosen every step of my career by that standard.</p>\n\n<p>There is one more reason I keep choosing the launch phase. When a new trend emerges, its leading edge attracts talented people who love new things. Learning the latest technology, implementing it, and seeing customers happy is enjoyable in itself. But working alongside those people is the particular pleasure of the early days.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” Given that, why Sakana AI?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Because I thought it was the best AI company in Japan. It is growing fast as a startup, its valuation is rising rapidly, and it is a Japan-born company aiming at the global market with research and development as its foundation. I had been following the company for some time. Then an acquaintance introduced me, and I had the chance to actually hear the story.</p>\n\n<p>Before joining, I spoke with Ren Ito, co-founder and chairman. Hearing his vision of scaling Sakana AI as a business not just in Japan but globally was exciting. The founders and the members are all wonderful people, and in the end, the desire to work with good people was what decided it.</p>\n\n<p>Because the company started from R&amp;D, it has excellent technology and attracts talented people. But turning that into something the world can use is not easy. I felt the company was building a team for exactly that and expanding rapidly, and the GTM team now owns that part: making it usable.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"from-research-insights-to-products-that-reach-more-customers\"><strong>From Research Insights to Products That Reach More Customers</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â€” Letâ€™s talk about that mission of making the technology usable. Where does the GTM team stand today?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Alongside its research, Sakana AI has focused on industries such as finance and defense and implemented AI on the ground at customers. We have now entered a phase of turning the knowledge accumulated there into products and delivering them to a broader range of customers.</p>\n\n<p>That said, releasing a product does not mean customers can use it right away. There will always be situations where the product alone cannot meet a customerâ€™s requirements. To close that gap, we are currently hiring Forward Deployed Engineers (FDEs).</p>\n\n<p>FDEs feed what they learn at the customerâ€™s site back into the product. As that accumulates, we want to increase the number of cases where the product can be used on its own, without an FDE.</p>\n\n<p>Many AI companies are investing in the FDE role right now. At the same time, people sometimes ask how it differs from an engineer who goes on site and builds things for the customer. The difference is precisely this feedback loop. Solving the customerâ€™s problem is not the end. Reflecting that experience back into product improvement is part of the job. That is the role of an FDE at Sakana AI and the model we are aiming for.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"the-product-opens-the-conversation-the-customers-problem-is-what-we-solve\"><strong>The Product Opens the Conversation; the Customerâ€™s Problem Is What We Solve</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â€” The job descriptions for the GTM team use the phrase â€œproduct-driven GTM.â€� It sounds different from both system-integrator development and deploying a SaaS product as is. What is the approach?</strong></p>\n\n<p>When people hear â€œproduct-driven,â€� many picture a one-way push to sell a product that already exists. Our stance is a little different. For us, product-driven means using the product as the starting point for a conversation with the customer. We present the product first, watch the customerâ€™s reaction, and then propose the best way to apply it as a solution. It is less about going out to sell a product and more about matching the product to the customerâ€™s problem.</p>\n\n<p>There are also cases where the customer does not know how to implement it, or where security has to be tailored to specific requirements. These requirements differ by industry and by company, so an FDE steps in to move the deployment forward.</p>\n\n<p>When there are no special requirements, customers can use the product as is. Our API services, for example, are called directly from the customerâ€™s own systems. Sakana Marlin is a research product for business users, aimed mainly at people who do research work every day.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” We understand you are not selling only through your own team, but also place importance on working with partners.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Our resources are limited, so partners are essential. One type is system integrators, whom we are encouraging to build Sakana AI products into their own solutions. The other is technology companies with partner ecosystems, where Sakana AI products are offered through their marketplaces.</p>\n\n<p>This model lets us grow the user base not just in Japan but globally.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"orchestrating-multiple-models-and-the-growing-demand-for-ai-sovereignty\"><strong>Orchestrating Multiple Models, and the Growing Demand for AI Sovereignty</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â€” A bit more on products. Orchestration, which brings in multiple frontier models, is one of Sakana AIâ€™s technical strengths. How are customers responding?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Many companies have recently released features that let you switch between different models. Sakana Fugu does not simply switch models, though. It takes the approach of having multiple frontier models work together on a single task. I think that is a very distinctive feature.</p>\n\n<p>Behind the demand for this approach is a wariness about continuing to depend on a single model, however good it is. In February 2026, the US government instructed federal agencies to stop using Anthropic products, and the US Department of Defense designated the company a supply chain risk. No matter how strong the performance, a model can become unavailable for political or contractual reasons. Many companies must have felt that firsthand.</p>\n\n<p>More and more companies want to use multiple models, and this approach is drawing attention. We also hear concerns that a particular model could suddenly become unavailable. For better or worse, these circumstances are a tailwind, and we are receiving inquiries from many customers.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/assets/vp-of-gtm-interview/fugu-orchestration.png\" width=\"100%\" /><br /></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” Are you seeing changes in customer attitudes around AI sovereignty as well?</strong></p>\n\n<p>I think so. AI is a bigger trend than anything before it, and precisely because of that, awareness of security is extremely high. Awareness of AI sovereignty and data sovereignty is rising among both customers themselves and governments, and requests of this kind are clearly increasing.</p>\n\n<p>And it is not limited to large companies. Small and medium-sized businesses are also interested from a data sovereignty perspective. It has become a theme shared across a wide range of customers.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” Demand is high, yet both customers and sellers still seem to be feeling their way. Compared with cloud and IoT, which you have worked on before, what is the same and what is different?</strong></p>\n\n<p>First, the AI market is overwhelmingly larger. Beyond that, the customerâ€™s situation is different. Cloud had an easy-to-understand framing as a replacement for on-premises. IoT had M2M communications before it, so customers understood it quickly, and the decision was often simply to migrate if it improved efficiency.</p>\n\n<p>AI is not like that. The customers themselves are feeling their way. They do not know what to do. And because information from around the world now arrives flat over the internet, AI players and customers are struggling at the same time. That makes the job of selling harder.</p>\n\n<p>Still, customers have a strong sense that they have to do something. We can approach them exactly where they do not know how, so the business is moving at tremendous speed. On the other hand, models are evolving so quickly that customers tend to avoid long-term commitments. They also want an environment they can use with confidence and safety.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” In that market, where do you aim to be in one year and in three?</strong></p>\n\n<p>The immediate priority is to build up effective deployment results with customers in Japan. But when it comes to expanding globally, waiting until we have a track record in Japan and then taking it overseas would be too slow. So we are moving with the intention of pursuing Japan and overseas in parallel from the start.</p>\n\n<p>In Japan, we will expand broadly through collaboration with the investors who have backed us and through partnerships with a range of partners. Overseas, the thinking is the same: build results with the overseas big tech companies and other investors who have backed us, and scale from there. Ideally, in three years, overseas will account for the larger share.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"we-want-people-who-enjoy-an-environment-that-changes-fast\"><strong>We Want People Who Enjoy an Environment That Changes Fast</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â€” Letâ€™s turn to the team. The GTM team is hiring for several roles: Account Executive, FDE, Partner Alliances, and Customer Success. For each role, what kind of person do you want to join?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Let me start with what applies to every role. One is enjoying change. Both the industry and the company are moving very dramatically right now, and we want people who can enjoy that and thrive in it. The other is curiosity. Building your work while engaging with a wide range of people, including customers, the industry, and colleagues inside the company, requires curiosity.</p>\n\n<p>On top of that, for Account Executives, the market is large and we expect the deal sizes we sell to grow dynamically. We are looking for people who have built large businesses from zero and closed large deals. Partner Alliances handles the collaboration with system integrators and ecosystems I mentioned earlier. It is a job of designing from scratch who we partner with and how we sell together.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” What kind of person are you looking for as an FDE?</strong></p>\n\n<p>For FDEs, I do not want them to start from â€œwe canâ€™tâ€� when a customer makes a request. The role is to look at the customerâ€™s requirements against our capabilities from a technical standpoint and close the gap between them. Someone who can carry a problem through from discovery to resolution on the technical side is a good fit.</p>\n\n<p>Beyond that, we want people who can look broadly for the next problem and the next opportunity, even outside their scope. Do not stop at solving the issue. Find what is needed next and connect it to product improvement. That is an FDE at Sakana AI.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><strong>â€” Customer Success, a new role you are hiring for, handles customers after deployment, correct?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Yes, it handles follow-up after a customer has deployed. Because models evolve quickly, we need to keep checking whether customers are using them properly and whether new issues have emerged. We will be releasing products aggressively from here on, so combining them into a more efficient setup for the customer is also part of Customer Successâ€™s job.</p>\n\n<p>By the nature of the role, Account Executives move on to the next customer. If deployed customers are left alone, they may switch to another company or simply lose track of what to do. Supporting them there is a very important role.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/assets/vp-of-gtm-interview/datadog-event.jpg\" width=\"100%\" /><br /></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><small><i>Speaking at a Datadog event, October 2026</i></small></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"now-is-the-only-time-you-can-build-the-system-with-us\"><strong>Now Is the Only Time You Can Build the System With Us</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â€” What can you gain by joining the GTM team now?</strong></p>\n\n<p>There is still a great deal that has not been systematized, so you get to build those systems with us. That is the biggest thing.</p>\n\n<p>Members from a range of backgrounds are joining, each bringing their own ways of thinking and opinions to the discussion. That produces new insights, and moments where you realize what it means to build something new. Opportunities for this kind of experience are rare. Building from zero at a company riding the big wave of AI is a valuable experience.</p>\n\n<p>And then, from Japan to the world. This is a challenge only Sakana AI can offer. Sakana AIâ€™s development base is in Tokyo. The researchers and the product development team sit at the desks right next to the GTM team. Feedback and feature requests from customers can be discussed with the development side on the spot and reflected in the product right away. There is no waiting for a reply after raising a request to an overseas headquarters.</p>\n\n<p>We want to deliver technology and products born in Japan to customers around the world at this same speed. We are waiting for people who will build that with us.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"careers\">Careers</h2>\n\n<p>Delivering Japan-born AI products to customers around the world. The work of building the system to do that has only just begun. If you are interested in Sakana AIâ€™s GTM team, please visit our <a href=\"https://sakana.ai/careers/?category=gtm\">careers page</a>.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/assets/careers/global-account-executive.jpg\" width=\"100%\" /><br /></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h1 id=\"Japanese\">æ—¥æœ¬ç™ºã�®AIãƒ—ãƒ­ãƒ€ã‚¯ãƒˆã‚’ä¸–ç•Œã�«å£²ã‚‹ï¼šSakana AI GTMè²¬ä»»è€…ã‚¤ãƒ³ã‚¿ãƒ“ãƒ¥ãƒ¼</h1>\n\n<p><img src=\"/assets/vp-of-gtm-interview/thumbnail.jpg\" width=\"100%\" /><br /></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p>Sakana AIã�¯2026å¹´ã€�Sakana Chatã€�Sakana Marlinã€�Sakana Fuguã€�Sakana Translateã�ªã�©ã€�ãƒ—ãƒ­ãƒ€ã‚¯ãƒˆã‚’ç›¸æ¬¡ã�„ã�§ãƒªãƒªãƒ¼ã‚¹ã�—ã�¾ã�—ã�Ÿã€‚ç ”ç©¶é–‹ç™ºä¼�æ¥­ã�¨ã�—ã�¦çŸ¥ã‚‰ã‚Œã�¦ã��ã�ŸSakana AIã�¯ã€�ç ”ç©¶ã�‹ã‚‰ç”Ÿã�¾ã‚Œã�Ÿãƒ—ãƒ­ãƒ€ã‚¯ãƒˆã‚’ä¼�æ¥­ã‚„å®˜å…¬åº�ã�«å±Šã�‘ã‚‹æ®µéš�ã�«å…¥ã�£ã�¦ã�„ã�¾ã�™ã€‚ã��ã�®è²©å£²ã�¨æ‹¡å¤§ã‚’æ‹…ã�†GTMï¼ˆGo-To-Marketï¼‰ãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã�Œã€�2026å¹´9æœˆã�«æœ¬æ ¼çš„ã�«ç«‹ã�¡ä¸Šã�Œã‚Šã�¾ã�—ã�Ÿã€‚</p>\n\n<p>ãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã‚’ç�‡ã�„ã‚‹ã�®ã�¯ã€�ã‚¯ãƒ©ã‚¦ãƒ‰ã€�IoTã€�ãƒ‰ãƒ­ãƒ¼ãƒ³ã�¨ã�„ã�£ã�Ÿæ–°ã�—ã�„æŠ€è¡“ã�Œæ–°ã�Ÿã�ªç”£æ¥­ã‚’å½¢æˆ�ã�™ã‚‹ã€�ã��ã�®é»�æ˜�æœŸã�‹ã‚‰äº‹æ¥­ã�®ç«‹ã�¡ä¸Šã�’ã‚’ç‰½å¼•ã�—ã�¦ã��ã�ŸæŸ¿å³¶ã�•ã‚“ã�§ã�™ã€‚ã�ªã�œä»Šã€�Sakana AIã�ªã�®ã�‹ã€‚ç ”ç©¶ã�‹ã‚‰ç”Ÿã�¾ã‚Œã�Ÿãƒ—ãƒ­ãƒ€ã‚¯ãƒˆã‚’ã�©ã�†å£²ã‚Šã€�ã�©ã‚“ã�ªãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã�§åºƒã�’ã�¦ã�„ã��ã�®ã�‹ã€‚ã��ã�—ã�¦ã€�ã�¾ã� ä»•çµ„ã�¿ã�®ã�ªã�„ã�“ã�®ãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã�«ä»Šå…¥ã‚‹é�¢ç™½ã�•ã�¯ã�©ã�“ã�«ã�‚ã‚‹ã�®ã�‹ã�«ã�¤ã�„ã�¦è��ã��ã�¾ã�—ã�Ÿã€‚</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"ã‚¤ãƒ³ã‚¿ãƒ“ãƒ¥ãƒ¼ã‚¤ãƒ¼\">ã‚¤ãƒ³ã‚¿ãƒ“ãƒ¥ãƒ¼ã‚¤ãƒ¼</h2>\n\n<table style=\"background-color: #FFF;border: 1px solid white;border-collapse: collapse;margin:0;padding:0;\">\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"45%\" style=\"background-color: #FFF;border: 1px solid white;border-collapse: collapse;margin:0;padding:0.5em;\">\n<img src=\"/assets/vp-of-gtm-interview/headshot.jpg\" width=\"100%\" /><br />\n</td>\n<td width=\"55%\" style=\"background-color: #FFF;border: 1px solid white;border-collapse: collapse;margin:0;padding:0.5em;\">\n<b>æŸ¿å³¶ è‹±å’Œ</b><br />Hidekazu Kakishima<br />VP of GTM<br /><br />\nAWSã�®æ—¥æœ¬æ³•äººç«‹ã�¡ä¸Šã�’æœŸã�«æˆ¦ç•¥çš„ã‚¢ã‚«ã‚¦ãƒ³ãƒˆãƒ�ãƒ¼ãƒ ã‚’ç�‡ã�„ã€�ã��ã�®å¾ŒIoTé€šä¿¡ã‚¹ã‚¿ãƒ¼ãƒˆã‚¢ãƒƒãƒ—SORACOMã�®å‰µæ¥­ãƒ¡ãƒ³ãƒ�ãƒ¼ã€�ç±³å›½ãƒ‰ãƒ­ãƒ¼ãƒ³ãƒ¡ãƒ¼ã‚«ãƒ¼Skydioã�§ãƒ•ãƒ©ã‚¤ãƒ³ã‚°ãƒ­ãƒœãƒ†ã‚£ã‚¯ã‚¹ã�®ç¤¾ä¼šå®Ÿè£…ã‚’æ�¨é€²ã€‚ç�¾åœ¨ã�¯Sakana AIã�«ã�Šã�„ã�¦ãƒ—ãƒ­ãƒ€ã‚¯ãƒˆç¾¤ã�®å¸‚å ´å±•é–‹ã�¨ä¾¡å€¤æ��ä¾›ã�«è²¬ä»»ã‚’æŒ�ã�¤ã€‚\n<p><br /></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<h2 id=\"æŠ€è¡“ã�®æœ€å…ˆç«¯ã�«ã�¯æ–°ã�—ã�„ã‚‚ã�®å¥½ã��ã�®å„ªç§€ã�ªäººã�Œé›†ã�¾ã‚‹\"><strong>æŠ€è¡“ã�®æœ€å…ˆç«¯ã�«ã�¯æ–°ã�—ã�„ã‚‚ã�®å¥½ã��ã�®å„ªç§€ã�ªäººã�Œé›†ã�¾ã‚‹</strong></h2>\n\n<p><strong>â”€â