†ã�‹ã�§ã€�å�Œã�˜è³ªå•�ã�¸ã�®ç­”ã�ˆã�¯å¤§ã��ã��å¤‰ã‚�ã‚Šã�¾ã�™ã€‚æ¯�å›�å�Œã�˜èª¬æ˜�ã‚’ç¹°ã‚Šè¿”ã�•ã�ªã��ã�¦ã‚‚ã€�å‰�å›�ã�®ç¶šã��ã�‹ã‚‰è©±ã�›ã‚‹ã�“ã�¨ã€‚Sakana Chatã�Œæ—¥ã€…ã�®é ¼ã‚Œã‚‹å­˜åœ¨ã�«ã�ªã‚‹ã�Ÿã‚�ã�«ã�¯ã€�ãƒ¡ãƒ¢ãƒªã�Œæ¬ ã�‹ã�›ã�¾ã�›ã‚“ã€‚</p>\n\n<p>My Fishã�§ãƒ¡ãƒ¢ãƒªã‚’è¦‹ã�ˆã‚‹å½¢ã�«ã�—ã�Ÿã�®ã�¯ã€�ã��ã�®æœ€åˆ�ã�®ä¸€æ­©ã�§ã�™ã€‚ã�“ã‚Œã�‹ã‚‰ã�¯ã€�è¦šã�ˆã‚‹ã�¹ã��ã�“ã�¨ã‚’ã‚ˆã‚Šçš„ç¢ºã�«è¦‹ã��ã‚�ã‚�ã€�çŠ¶æ³�ã�®å¤‰åŒ–ã�«å�ˆã‚�ã�›ã�¦å†…å®¹ã‚’æ›´æ–°ã�—ã€�é•·ã��ä½¿ã�†ã�»ã�©ã�‚ã�ªã�Ÿã�«å�ˆã�£ã�Ÿå½¢ã�«è‚²ã�£ã�¦ã�„ã��ã€‚ã��ã‚“ã�ªãƒ¡ãƒ¢ãƒªã‚’ç›®æŒ‡ã�—ã�¦æ”¹å–„ã‚’ç¶šã�‘ã�¦ã�„ã��ã�¾ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p>Sakana Chatã�«ã€�æ¬¡ã�¯ã�©ã‚“ã�ªã�“ã�¨ã�Œã�§ã��ã‚‹ã‚ˆã�†ã�«ã�ªã�£ã�¦ã�»ã�—ã�„ã�§ã�™ã�‹ï¼Ÿ ã�œã�²ã�”æ„�è¦‹ã‚’ã�Šè��ã�‹ã�›ã��ã� ã�•ã�„ã€‚</p>\n\n<p>My Fishã‚’è¦‹ã�¦ã�¿ã‚‹ï¼šWebãƒ»ãƒ¢ãƒ�ã‚¤ãƒ«ã�¨ã‚‚ã�«ã€�ã‚µã‚¤ãƒ‰ãƒ�ãƒ¼ä¸‹ã�®ã‚¢ã‚«ã‚¦ãƒ³ãƒˆã‚’ã‚¯ãƒªãƒƒã‚¯ã�—ã€�<strong>ã€Œè¨­å®šã€�â†’ã€Œãƒ¡ãƒ¢ãƒªã€�â†’ã€ŒMy Fishã�§ãƒ¡ãƒ¢ãƒªã‚’è¦‹ã‚‹ã€�</strong>ã�‹ã‚‰é–‹ã�‘ã�¾ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p>Sakana Chatã�¯<a href=\"https://chat.sakana.ai/\">https://chat.sakana.ai/</a>ã�‹ã‚‰ã�”åˆ©ç”¨ã�„ã�Ÿã� ã�‘ã�¾ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sakana-ai\"><strong>Sakana AI</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Sakana AIã�¯ã€�AIã�®æœªæ�¥ã‚’ç§�ã�Ÿã�¡ã�¨ä¸€ç·’ã�«åˆ‡ã‚Šæ‹“ã�„ã�¦ã��ã� ã�•ã‚‹æ–¹ã‚’å‹Ÿé›†ã�—ã�¦ã�„ã�¾ã�™ã€‚å½“ç¤¾ã�®<a href=\"/careers/\">å‹Ÿé›†è¦�é …</a>ã‚’ã�”è¦§ã��ã� ã�•ã�„ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<a href=\"/careers/\"><img src=\"/assets/azabudai-hills/fish-outside-hills.jpeg\" style=\"width: 80%;\" /></a><br />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<hr />\n\n<p><h id=\"English\" style=\"color:#FFF;\">English Announcement</h></p>\n\n<h1 id=\"meet-my-fish-see-what-sakana-chat-remembers-about-you\">Meet My Fish: See what Sakana Chat remembers about you</h1>\n\n<center>\n<video src=\"/assets/my-fish/connections-en.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\" autoplay=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" loop=\"\" style=\"margin: 0; width: 100%;\"></video>\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p><em>A clearer, more visual way to understand the memories behind your conversations.</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>ğŸ‘‰Try Sakana Chatï¼š <a href=\"https://chat.sakana.ai/\">https://chat.sakana.ai/</a></strong></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<p>Have you ever mentioned something to Sakana Chat once, then had it resurface days later at exactly the right moment?</p>\n\n<p>Thatâ€™s memory at work. Until now, it has mostly stayed behind the scenes. With <strong>My Fish</strong>, you can finally see what Sakana Chat remembers about you, when those memories are used, and how they connect.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<video src=\"/assets/my-fish/overview-en.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\" autoplay=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" loop=\"\" style=\"margin: 0; width: 100%;\"></video>\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-fish-that-grows-with-every-conversation\"><strong>A fish that grows with every conversation</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Every conversation feeds your fish a little. Over time, it learns the details that make Sakana Chat more useful to you: how you like your answers, what youâ€™re working on, and the people, places, and projects that matter to you.</p>\n\n<p>My Fish gives those memories a home. Itâ€™s a place to watch your fish grow and make sure what it has learned still feels right.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-details-worth-remembering\"><strong>The details worth remembering</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Memory helps Sakana Chat carry useful context from one conversation to the next. Rather than keeping every detail, it focuses on information that may continue to be helpful, such as:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>How you prefer Sakana Chat to respond</li>\n  <li>A project or goal youâ€™re working toward</li>\n  <li>Useful details about you</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"see-recent-activity-at-a-glance\"><strong>See recent activity at a glance</strong></h2>\n\n<p>At the top of My Fish, a heatmap shows when Sakana Chat used your memories and when it learned something new. It remains visible as you move between views, giving you a quick sense of how your memories are being used.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<img class=\"b-lazy\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAAAAACH5BAEKAAEALAAAAAABAAEAAAICTAEAOw==\" data-src=\"/assets/my-fish/heatmap-en.png\" style=\"width: 100%;\" />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"discover-what-your-fish-remembers\"><strong>Discover what your fish remembers</strong></h2>\n\n<p>The <strong>Overview</strong> brings all your memories together in one place. Theyâ€™re grouped into helpful categories, such as your profile, preferences, current projects, and favorite topics, so itâ€™s easy to understand what your fish knows.</p>\n\n<p>For each memory, you can see how many facts it contains, how often it has been read, and when it was last used. Open one to explore the individual facts and the conversations they came from. You can even jump straight back to the original chat.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<img class=\"b-lazy\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAAAAACH5BAEKAAEALAAAAAABAAEAAAICTAEAOw==\" data-src=\"/assets/my-fish/overview-en.png\" style=\"width: 100%;\" />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"explore-how-your-memories-connect\"><strong>Explore how your memories connect</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Switch to <strong>Connections</strong> to see your memories arranged as a living map around your fish.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Closer memories are more recent.</strong> The nearer a memory is to the center, the more recently it was used.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bigger memories are used more often.</strong> The size of each node reflects how frequently that memory has been used.</li>\n  <li><strong>Faded memories may be getting stale.</strong> A faded node hasnâ€™t been used in 30 days or more.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chords reveal relationships.</strong> Lines connect memories that appeared together in the same reply, showing which ideas Sakana Chat tends to combine.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Select any node to see its facts, when it was learned, where it was used, and which memories often appear alongside it. Each related memory is a link, so you can follow the connections and wander through the map. Select the fish in the center to open your profile.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<video src=\"/assets/my-fish/connections-en.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\" autoplay=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" loop=\"\" style=\"margin: 0; width: 100%;\"></video>\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"look-back-at-what-your-fish-learned\"><strong>Look back at what your fish learned</strong></h2>\n\n<p>The <strong>History</strong> view provides a detailed timeline of every read and write. Expand any write to see exactly which facts were added.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<img class=\"b-lazy\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAAAAACH5BAEKAAEALAAAAAABAAEAAAICTAEAOw==\" data-src=\"/assets/my-fish/history-en.png\" style=\"width: 100%;\" />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"notice-memory-at-work-while-you-chat\"><strong>Notice memory at work while you chat</strong></h2>\n\n<p>You donâ€™t need to open Settings to see memory in action. Whenever Sakana Chat reads or saves a memory while composing a response, its thinking process now displays a small chip. For example, you might see â€œChecked memory: Trip to Spain, Hikingâ€� or â€œLearned something new: Osaka marathon.â€�</p>\n\n<p>Select a chip and My Fish opens directly to that memory, giving you instant context without interrupting your conversation.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<video src=\"/assets/my-fish/chip-en.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\" autoplay=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" loop=\"\" style=\"margin: 0; width: 100%;\"></video>\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"take-your-fish-with-you\"><strong>Take your fish with you</strong></h2>\n\n<p>My Fish is designed for mobile too. In the Connections map, selecting a node opens a drawer from the bottom while the graph shifts to keep your chosen memory in view. Swipe up for full details, or select another node to keep exploring.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<video src=\"/assets/my-fish/mobile-en.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\" autoplay=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" loop=\"\" style=\"margin: 0; width: 100%;\"></video>\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"this-is-only-the-beginning\"><strong>This is only the beginning</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Making memory visible is the first step. Next, weâ€™re exploring more intelligent ways to help memories take shape, evolve, and stay useful over time.</p>\n\n<p>What would you like your fish to do next? Weâ€™d love to hear from you.</p>\n\n<p>Ready to meet your My Fish? Click your account at the bottom of the sidebar, then open <strong>Settings â†’ Memory â†’ â€œView memories in My Fishâ€�</strong> on web or mobile.</p>\n\n<p>Sakana Chat is available at <a href=\"https://chat.sakana.ai/\">https://chat.sakana.ai/</a></p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sakana-ai-1\"><strong>Sakana AI</strong></h2>\n\n<p>Interested in joining us?</p>\n\n<p>Please see our <a href=\"/careers/\">career opportunities</a> for more information.</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<center>\n<a href=\"/careers/\"><img src=\"/assets/azabudai-hills/fish-outside-hills.jpeg\" style=\"width: 80%;\" /></a><br />\n</center>\n\n<p><br /></p>]]></content><author><name>Sakana AI</name></author><category term=\"media\" /><summary type=\"html\"><![CDATA[]]></summary></entry><entry xml:lang=\"en\"><title type=\"html\">Recursive Self-Improvement through Collective Intelligence</title><link href=\"/mass/\" rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" title=\"Recursive Self-Improvement through Collective Intelligence\" /><published>2026-10-12T00:00:00+09:00</published><updated>2026-10-12T00:00:00+09:00</updated><id>/mass</id><content type=\"html\" xml:base=\"/mass/\"><![CDATA[<center>\n<video class=\"b-lazy\" autoplay=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" loop=\"\" style=\"display: block; margin: auto; width: 80%;\"><source src=\"/assets/pub/mass.mp4\" /></video>\n</center>\n<!--more-->\n\n<p>As AI systems tackle increasingly open-ended research problems, human experts may struggle to assess results and guide further progress. The model itself may be the best available optimizer and evaluator. The challenge is obtaining useful self-supervision within that homogeneous loop, where a model risks reinforcing its own blind spots rather than correcting them.</p>\n\n<p>We ask whether a model can learn from the collective intelligence of its own team.</p>\n\n<p>Introducing our new paper, <strong>Recursive Self-Improvement through Multi-Agent Self-Supervision</strong>:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Detailed Blog: <a href=\"https://pub.sakana.ai/mass/\">https://pub.sakana.ai/mass/</a></li>\n  <li>Paper: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12176\">https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12176</a></li>\n  <li>Code: <a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/mass\">https://github.com/SakanaAI/mass</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>We introduce Multi-Agent Self-Supervision (MASS). One shared model solves tasks through a team of virtual subagents, searches for better multi-agent workflows, then uses its own judgments to select the best ones. Training on the best teamâ€™s executions distills the teamâ€™s collective experience back into the shared model.</p>\n\n<p>We ran two cycles using a 27B open-weights model on synthetic open-ended research tasks. After the second cycle, score per output token reached 1.2-1.6x the base modelâ€™s level across four research benchmarks.</p>\n\n<p>Three key results:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>MASS enables role generalization within an RSI loop: training only on task-solving data from the optimizee naturally improves the capabilities of both the optimizer and the evaluator.</li>\n  <li>multi-agent trajectories are more efficient training data than single-agent trajectories for RSI.</li>\n  <li>optimizer capacity, rather than evaluator capacity, can be the bottleneck in the speed of improvement.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>We believe the community needs to investigate homogeneous RSI carefully to support AI safety. When a model is also its own evaluator, shared blind spots could allow mistakes to be accepted as progress and reinforced through training. For AI to improve beyond human expertise safely, we need to understand these failures and preserve effective ways to detect them and intervene.</p>\n\n<p>This work was done by Hyunin Lee, Jinglue Xu, Jeffrey Seely, Yujin Tang, and our collaborators from UC Berkeley (Somayeh Sojoudi, Matei Zaharia, Donghyun Lee).</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>]]></content><author><name>Sakana AI</name></author><category term=\"media\" /><summary type=\"html\"><![CDATA[]]></summary></entry><entry xml:lang=\"ja\"><title type=\"html\">åŒ»å¸«å�‘ã�‘æ¤œç´¢ãƒ„ãƒ¼ãƒ«ã€ŒEvidence Finderã€�ã�«Sakana Namazuã‚’å°�å…¥ï¼šæ—¥æœ¬ä»•æ§˜ã�®AIã‚’ã€�åŒ»ç™‚ã�®ç�¾å ´ã�¸</title><link href=\"/namazu-aillis/\" rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" title=\"åŒ»å¸«å�‘ã�‘æ¤œç´¢ãƒ„ãƒ¼ãƒ«ã€ŒEvidence Finderã€�ã�«Sakana Namazuã‚’å°�å…¥ï¼šæ—¥æœ¬ä»•æ§˜ã�®AIã‚’ã€�åŒ»ç™‚ã�®ç�¾å ´ã�¸\" /><published>2026-10-09T00:00:00+09:00</published><updated>2026-10-09T00:00:00+09:00</updated><id>/namazu-aillis</id><content type=\"html\" xml:base=\"/namazu-aillis/\"><![CDATA[<p><img class=\"b-lazy\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAAAAACH5BAEKAAEALAAAAAABAAEAAAICTAEAOw==\" data-src=\"/assets/namazu-aillis/namazu-aillis.png\" style=\"width: 100%;\" /><br /></p>\n\n<!--more-->\n\n<hr />\n\n<p>Sakana AIã�®é–‹ç™ºã�™ã‚‹æ—¥æœ¬ä»•æ§˜ã�®LLMã€Œ<a href=\"/namazu/\">Sakana Namazu</a>ã€�ã�Œã€�åŒ»å¸«å�‘ã�‘ã‚¨ãƒ“ãƒ‡ãƒ³ã‚¹æ¤œç´¢ãƒ„ãƒ¼ãƒ«ã€Œ<a href=\"https://overview.evidencefinder.jp/\">Evidence Finder</a>ã€�ã�«æ�¡ç”¨ã�•ã‚Œã�¾ã�—ã�Ÿã€‚Evidence Finderã�¯ã€�åŒ»ç™‚AIã‚’æ‰‹ã�Œã�‘ã‚‹ã‚¢ã‚¤ãƒªã‚¹æ ªå¼�ä¼šç¤¾ã�Œæ��ä¾›ã�™ã‚‹ã‚µãƒ¼ãƒ“ã‚¹ã�§ã�™ã€‚</p>\n\n<p><br /></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"åŒ»å¸«ã�Œã‚¨ãƒ“ãƒ‡ãƒ³ã‚¹ã�¸é€Ÿã‚„ã�‹ã�«åˆ°é�”ã�§ã��ã‚‹å°�ç·šã‚’æ��ä¾›ã�™ã‚‹\"><strong>åŒ»å¸«ã�Œã‚¨ãƒ“ãƒ‡ãƒ³ã‚¹ã�¸é€Ÿã‚„ã�‹ã�«åˆ°é�”ã�§ã��ã‚‹å°�ç·šã‚’æ��ä¾›ã�™ã‚‹</strong></h2>\n\n<p>åŒ»ç™‚ã�®ç�¾å ´ã�§ã�¯ã€�æ—¥ã€…æ›´æ–°ã�•ã‚Œã‚‹è«–æ–‡ã‚„è¨ºç™‚ã